fact WorkItem(id: "WI-981", description: some(value: "R4's CAPTURE CLAUSE (proposal 059) is a change to behaviour the spec currently\nPERMITS. Measure the migration before implementing it.\n\nMUST AGREE WITH docs/proposals/059-secondary-entries.md R4, third clause, which\ndefines the rule: no declaration may CAPTURE a name that already resolves in the\nsort's scope. Do not re-decide the policy here; if the migration cost makes the\nrule untenable, amend 059 rather than diverging from it.\n\nTHE HAZARD, MEASURED (all three rows drive `Rec(n: 0).use()` after load):\n\n  namespace lib\n    operation f(x: Int64) -> Int64 = 1\n  end\n  namespace app\n    import lib.f\n    sort Rec\n      entity Rec(n: Int64)\n      operation use(r: Rec) -> Int64 = f(r.n)    -- bare `f` -> the import\n    end\n    ...\n  end\n\n  base, nothing else added                        => 1\n  + `namespace Rec  operation f(...) = 2  end`    => 2   (a SECOND ENTRY)\n  + the same operation in Rec's OWN body          => 2   (the CONTROL)\n\nThe unedited body changed meaning in both cases. Note the control: this is NOT\ncreated by secondary entries, so the fix belongs to the SCOPE, not to secondary entries --\nan entry-local patch would be aimed at the wrong object and would split the one\nshared scope 059 R2 rests on.\n\nALREADY KNOWN, AS A CAUTION: kernel-language.md §6.3 records exactly this for the\nlong form (WI-935) -- \"Mind the short name ... the new member is a second binding\nfor it, and an `import ns.{f}` elsewhere may bind to the member rather than to\nthe namespace-level `f` -- silently, since both are legal (measured)\". 059 R4\nturns that caution into a refusal, for both spellings at once.\n\nALSO COVERED by the same clause, so the measurement must include them:\n  - IMPORTS are shared across all entries of a scope, MEASURED in both\n    directions: an import written in a secondary entry resolves a bare name in the\n    sort's BODY (=7, against a control with the import deleted that fails\n    `unknown functor`), and an import in the body resolves one in the entry (=9).\n    So an import is a capture route exactly as a declaration is.\n  - CONSTS capture too: a secondary entry `const K` shadows an imported `K` (=2,\n    control =1). (A const is NOT a member -- `X.K` and `receiver.K` resolve to\n    nothing in EITHER placement -- it is a name in the sort's scope. See 059 R2.)\n\nTHE MEASUREMENT THIS TICKET IS FOR, and it gates the rest: across stdlib,\nanthill-stl, examples/ and every fixture, count the declarations whose SHORT NAME\nalready resolves in the scope they are declared in -- i.e. the sites the new\nrefusal would reject. Report the count and the sites BEFORE changing behaviour.\nZero means the rule can simply land. Non-zero means it needs a migration and\npossibly a narrower rule (e.g. refuse only where the captured name is reached by\nIMPORT, not by an enclosing declaration), which is an amendment to 059.\n\nWATCH FOR a false positive that would inflate the count: a member legitimately\nsharing a spelling with something in an UNRELATED scope is not a capture. The\nquestion is only whether the name resolves at the declaring scope, by the same\nladder `resolve_in_scope` runs.\n\nACCEPTANCE: the count and the site list are reported first. Then, if the rule\nlands: a capturing declaration is a load error naming both the declaration and\nwhat it would capture; a test drives the three rows above (base => 1, second\nentry => refused, own body => refused) plus one row per capture route (operation,\nconst, import). Say at the test site which rows fail when the fix is backed out.\ncargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "codex", at: "2026-08-08T13:21:16Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-981", author: "user", content: "MEASURED (2026-08-04), the count this ticket exists for. Probe: for every\ndeclared symbol, ask what its short name would resolve to in its declaring scope\nwith step 1 (locals) skipped -- the same ladder `resolve_in_scope_recursive`\nruns, steps 1b and 2 only -- and record BY WHICH ROUTE the name was already\nreachable. Corpus: stdlib/anthill + rustland/anthill-stl/anthill + examples/ +\nanthill-todo, loaded as one program.\n\n  63 capture sites total   =   import 4  +  enclosing-scope 40  +  requires/provides 19\n  (stdlib alone: 56 = 4 + 33 + 19; examples/anthill-todo add 7, all enclosing)\n\nSO THE CLAUSE CANNOT LAND AS WRITTEN -- 63 refusals, including the stdlib's\ncentral pattern. Nor does the narrowing this ticket anticipated rescue it:\n\"import route only\" is 4 sites, and all four are `FiniteFilteredStream.collect`\n/ `FiniteMappedStream.iterator` and kin -- a sort declaring the operation of a\nspec whose name it imported. Legitimate overrides, every one.\n\nMOST OF THE 63 ARE NOT MEMBER DECLARATIONS AT ALL, and should never have been in\nscope:\n  - PARAMS. `Float.pow.exp`, `String.contains.sub`, `KB.assert.kb` and many more\n    are an operation's PARAMETER shadowing an operation name. A binder is not a\n    declaration.\n  - TYPE NAMES. `IndexedSeq.Effect` over `prelude.Effect`, `TypeExtractor.Error`\n    over `prelude.Error`, `logic.Classical.Classical` over its own namespace.\n    Dot dispatch never routes through these.\n\nRESTRICTED TO THE R4-RELEVANT SHAPE -- a declared OPERATION capturing an\nOPERATION:\n\n  25 total  =  import 4  +  enclosing-scope 2  +  requires/provides 19\n\n  23 of the 25 are the OVERRIDE pattern: a member implementing an operation of a\n  spec the sort requires/provides (`List.length` over `IndexedSeq.length`,\n  `List.head` over `Stream.head`, ...), reached either through the requires\n  parent link or through an import of the spec's member. Refusing these would\n  refuse the way the standard library is written.\n\n  EXACTLY 2 have no override relationship, and both are the P1a shape -- a sort's\n  operation shadowing a namespace-level operation of the same name:\n\n      anthill.reflect.KB.nonvar   captures  anthill.reflect.nonvar\n      anthill.reflect.KB.ground   captures  anthill.reflect.ground\n\nVERDICT. The only formulation with a defensible blast radius is: refuse a MEMBER\nOPERATION that captures an OPERATION with NO OVERRIDE RELATIONSHIP -- the\ncaptured name is not a member of a spec the declaring sort requires or provides,\nnor imported from one. That is 2 sites, both in anthill.reflect, and both would\nneed a decision (rename, or exempt as intentional) before it lands.\n\nWORTH WEIGHING BEFORE IMPLEMENTING: at 2 sites, a WARNING may be the right\ninstrument rather than a refusal -- WI-346 already warns for the neighbouring\nrequires-shadow case, and this would be its sibling. That is a call for the\nproposal (059 R4), not for this ticket; 059 has been updated with the numbers.\n\nNOT MEASURED: inline test fixtures. The corpus above is what is on disk; a\nfixture that captures would be found by the guard test when the rule lands, not\nby this count.", at: "2026-08-04T07:56:06Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-981", author: "user", content: "CLAUSES 1 AND 2 MEASURED TOO (2026-08-04) -- the earlier feedback covered only\nclause 3. Asked because \"is R4 implementable\" cannot be answered from clause 3\nalone.\n\nDETECTION PROBLEM FIRST: clauses 1 and 2 CANNOT be checked by walking symbols.\n`SymbolTable::define` MERGES two same-named declarations in one scope into one\nsymbol, so by the time symbols exist the duplication is gone -- the 63-site\nprobe walked symbols and therefore could not have seen a duplicate at all. They\nneed each DECLARATION recorded as pass 1 makes it, keyed on (scope_raw, local\nname). That is the same ledger R1 needs for duplicate TYPE declarations, so it\nis one piece of machinery serving both rules.\n\nMEASURED that way (recorders on the Item::Operation, Item::Const and\nItem::OperationBlock arms of scan_items_pass1), over stdlib + anthill-stl +\nexamples + anthill-todo:\n\n  427 operation/const declarations recorded\n  NAMES DECLARED MORE THAN ONCE IN ONE SCOPE: 0\n  (stdlib alone: 371 declarations, 0 duplicates)\n\nSo clauses 1 and 2 land with NO MIGRATION.\n\nHONEST CAVEAT: the zero is easy. The corpus contains no secondary entries at all\n(WI-975 measured that -- the only twice-declared names in the workspace are three\nhand-written test fixtures), so clause 2 has nothing to bite on yet. Clause 1\nalso covers two same-named operations in one MAIN entry, and that is genuinely\nzero.\n\nNOT COUNTED: operations declared inside `provides ... language ... end` blocks\nreach the KB by another path and were not recorded. If the ledger becomes the\nreal implementation, cover that arm too.\n\nCLAUSE 3, RESTATED FROM THE FIRST MEASUREMENT: it must key on the OVERRIDE\nRELATION -- is the captured operation a member of a spec the declaring sort\nrequires or provides -- and NOT on the route the name was reached by. 4 of the\nlegitimate overrides arrive through an `import` of the spec's member rather than\nthrough the `requires` link, so a route-based rule refuses exactly the wrong\nfour. The relation is known only after pass 2, so that check runs there, not in\npass 1 beside the ledger.\n\nSUMMARY FOR THE IMPLEMENTER:\n  clause 1  0 sites   pass-1 declaration ledger (shared with R1)\n  clause 2  0 sites   same ledger\n  clause 3  2 sites   override relation, post-pass-2; both sites in\n                      anthill.reflect need a verdict first", at: "2026-08-04T08:05:57Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-981", author: "user", content: "CLOSED 2026-08-08. The migration measurement is complete. Implementation is split among WI-1049 for duplicate operation declarations, WI-1000 for the secondary-entry body requirement, and WI-999 for capture under WI-1048 refinement semantics.", at: "2026-08-08T13:27:08Z")
